1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Here's a hard truth about prompt engineering. A prompt that produces one great answer hasn't

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
been proven. It's just gotten lucky one time. Real reliability comes from testing against a

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
fixed set of inputs and refining until the behavior holds. This lesson covers that loop

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and how to treat prompts like versioned code. Let's start with the loop itself, three moves

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
you cycle through. First, test. You run the prompt across a fixed set of inputs that reflect

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
what production actually sends, not one input you handpicked because you knew it would pass.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Second, observe. You look at where the outputs fail and you judge them against clear written

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
criteria, so you're measuring real behavior instead of admiring a single good answer.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Third, adjust. You change the prompt to fix the failures you saw, then run that same set again

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
to confirm the fix landed and didn't break anything else. Test, observe, adjust, and keep cycling

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
until the behavior holds across the whole set. Let me hammer on the mistake to watch because

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
almost everyone does it early on. You tweak a prompt, run it once, see a nice answer, and move on.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The problem is that one passing example tells you almost nothing about reliability. You got a good

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
result on one input. What about the other 50 shapes your app will actually see? Real evaluation

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
means judging the prompt across representative inputs, the everyday cases, and just as important,

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
the adversarial ones built specifically to break it. Proof isn't one win, it's consistent results

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
across the whole range, so resist the urge to ship on a single happy result. One lucky output

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
isn't validation. So what goes into that fixed set? Three kinds of input, and you want all three.

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
First, representative inputs. These are the everyday cases your application really sees,

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
and they confirm the prompt handles the common path well. Second, boundary inputs. The edges,

21
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
an empty field, an enormous input, an ambiguous request. These expose where the behavior starts

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:21,000
to bend before it breaks. Third, adversarial inputs. These are crafted to trip the prompt up

23
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
or to smuggle in instructions, and they test whether it holds under real pressure.

24
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Representative, boundary, adversarial. A set that covers all three tells you far more than a hundred

25
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
runs on the easy case. Let's contrast two ways of working because the difference is the whole lesson.

26
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The first is eyeballing. You change something, glance at one output, and call it good. The trouble

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,000
is that each change gets judged on a different input, whatever you happen to type that time,

28
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:58,000
so you genuinely can't tell what improved and what regressed. The second is a fixed eval set. You run

29
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
every version of the prompt against the same frozen inputs. Now a change is measurable. You can see it

30
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
fixed three failures and introduced none, or that it helped here and hurt there. That

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
comparability is what makes a change safe to keep or to roll back. Freeze the inputs and refinement

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
becomes real engineering. You met this pattern when we chained workflows, and here it becomes

33
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
your refinement engine. Draft, review, refine. Claude drafts an answer, a second call reviews it against

34
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
explicit criteria, and a third refines it based on that review. Picture it on a real task. The

35
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,000
draft call writes a summary, the review call checks it against your written criteria, every claim

36
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:49,000
sourced under 200 words, no invented numbers, and the refine call fixes exactly what the review flagged.

37
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Splitting the calls still buys you the same control. You can log each step, evaluate the intermediate

38
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
output, and branch when you need to. What's new here is a much lighter version of the same idea,

39
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,000
and that's where we go next. If a full chain feels heavy, there's a lighter version that costs almost

40
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
nothing. Add one line to the end of your prompt asking Claude to verify its answer against the

41
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:21,000
criteria before it finishes. That's it. This simple self-check catches errors surprisingly reliably,

42
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
and it's especially strong on the things models tend to slip on, like coding and math. You're

43
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:33,000
essentially giving the model a built-in second look before it commits to an answer. It's one

44
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
sentence of prompt, and it quietly turns a single forward pass into a quick draft and review of its

45
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
own work. When you need more reliability but don't want a multi-call pipeline, reach for the self-check

46
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
first. Now let's zoom out and treat the prompt the way it deserves, as a real artifact, not a string

47
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,000
you edit in place. Three habits here. First, version it. Keep your prompts in source control like any

48
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:10,000
other code so every change is reviewable, reproducible, and safe to roll back. Second, pin the model.

49
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Tie the prompt to a specific model ID because a new release can shift the behavior your prompt

50
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:24,000
was tuned against, and a floating alias will change under you silently. Third, regression test it. On

51
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:31,000
every change, rerun the prompt against your frozen eval set so a fix in one place doesn't quietly

52
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:39,000
break another. Version, pin, regression test. That's prompt engineering as engineering. Let me tie this

53
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:46,000
back to one word people throw around loosely, validated. A change that passed on a single happy

54
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
path example isn't validated. It's untested everywhere that matters. The mindset that keeps

55
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:59,000
you out of trouble is simple. Treat every prompt edit like a code change because it can regress

56
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
behavior exactly like code can. Gate it behind the same eval, set you test everything else on,

57
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
and only ship it when the whole set passes. Hard cases included. Not just the easy demo that

58
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
happened to look good. Validation is a bar the prompt has to clear. Not a single lucky run you

59
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:26,000
got excited about. Let's recap. Iterating against a fixed eval set is what turns a prompt from lucky

60
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:33,000
to reliable. One good output proves nothing on its own. Keep in mind a prompt edit is a code change

61
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
so version it, pin the model, and regression test before you ship.

62
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,000
That wraps up refining prompts against an eval set. I'll see you in the next one.

